WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.083
SPEAKER 1: One of the
common mitigation strategies

00:00:02.083 --> 00:00:04.950
we can use during an incident
is to simply add capacity

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.660
to handle unexpected
traffic or load.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.500
However, this only works if
there's capacity available.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.530
Running out a resource quota
can make a minor incident

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.730
into a major one.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:17.150
Today I'll show you how to
keep track of quota usage

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.990
in your projects with
Cloud monitoring.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.580
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.472
[THEME MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.210
Google Cloud enforces
quotas on resource usage

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.143
for project owners,
setting a hard limit

00:00:32.143 --> 00:00:34.185
on how much of a particular
Google Cloud resource

00:00:34.185 --> 00:00:35.710
your project can use.

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.780
Let's start by
reviewing the basics.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.680
There are two types of resource
usage that we limit with quota.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.160
The first one is rate quota,
such as API requests per day.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.300
This quota resets
after a specified time,

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.210
such as a minute or a day.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.250
The second one is
allocation quota,

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.430
such as the number of servicer
counts created in your project.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.170
This quota does
not reset over time

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.300
but must be explicitly
released, typically

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.463
by deleting the resource.

00:01:00.463 --> 00:01:01.880
You can see quotas
in your project

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.239
by going to the Quotas screen
under the IAM &amp; Admin menu.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:07.570
From there, you can see all
the quotas in your project

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.277
and how much of
them you're using.

00:01:09.277 --> 00:01:11.110
You can use this to
figure out if you're out

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:12.027
of a particular quota.

00:01:12.027 --> 00:01:13.570
And that's great,
but how can you

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.015
be more proactive about this
and prevent quota limits

00:01:16.015 --> 00:01:17.890
from getting in your
way, especially when you

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.483
need more capacity quickly?

00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:21.900
Well the good news
is that you can

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.640
get this information
in cloud monitoring

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.290
and even set up
alerts against it.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.210
Let's take a look.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.920
You can use the
Consumer Quota resource

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.365
to see the available metrics.

00:01:30.365 --> 00:01:31.740
There are two
quota usage metrics

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.440
for rate and allocation, there
is a metric for the quota

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.420
limit, and finally,
a metric showing you

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.340
how many times you've
actually hit that limit

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.370
called quota exceeded error.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.372
Start by selecting the
Quota Limit metric,

00:01:43.372 --> 00:01:45.330
then filter it through
just the Logging service

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.470
and the Write requests
per minute limit.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:49.950
In my project, that
limit is set to 60,000.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.462
That's a lot of logging.

00:01:51.462 --> 00:01:53.670
Now let's set another metric
showing how much of that

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.410
limit we're actually using.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.988
This time, use the Rate
quota usage metric,

00:01:57.988 --> 00:02:00.030
and again, filter it
through the logging service.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.890
In my case, I'm
nowhere near the limit.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.320
This is good news.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.380
Finally, you can configure
an alerting policy

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.100
which can notify you
if you actually hit

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.780
quota limits on your project.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.810
Use the Consumer Quota resource
in the condition configuration,

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.600
and select Quota exceeded
error as the metric.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.880
Now group it by service
and configure the alert

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.910
to fire if any time series
exceed the threshold.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.882
Now you'll know if you hit any
quota limits in the project.

00:02:24.882 --> 00:02:27.340
You should know that not all
services support quota metrics

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:28.110
at this point.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.860
Refer to the documentation
link that we'll

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.207
add to the show notes
for details on that.

00:02:32.207 --> 00:02:33.790
Monitoring quota
usage in your project

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.358
is a great way to proactively
make sure you always

00:02:36.358 --> 00:02:37.900
have the capacity
you need if there's

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.790
an unexpected spike in traffic.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:41.960
Thanks for watching and
come back soon for more.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:43.060
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.050
Stay healthy out there.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:47.400
[THEME MUSIC PLAYING]

